course: dictionary
lesson: 15209
title: A hard word about old age
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: Senile is an adjective. It describes a person whose mind has become weak because of old age, so they may forget things or get confused. Read the clues in the sentence: «My great-gran was senile and often forgot our names.» The words forgot and confused show what the word means.
explain: Senile has a second, old-fashioned meaning: it can mean connected with old age, as in «senile dementia». Today doctors say dementia instead, because senile is no longer the kind word to use.
explain: Be kind with this word. Calling a real person senile is unkind, even when you think it is a joke. «Going senile» is a phrase people use to tease, but it can hurt an older person's feelings. Before you use a word about someone, ask yourself: would I say this to their face?
fact: Senile is an adjective, so it describes a noun such as a person.
fact: «Senile» means weak in mind because of old age, especially forgetful or confused.
fact: «Dementia» is the modern word doctors use for this kind of memory and thinking loss.
fact: Calling someone senile is unkind, so be careful with this word.
fact: «Going senile» is a phrase people use, but as a joke about an older person it can hurt.
exercise: «My great-gran was senile and often forgot our names.» What does senile mean here? | Quick at running races | Very tall and strong | *Weak in mind because of old age, and forgetful | Happy to be going on holiday
exercise: The doctor said «senile dementia» makes people very confused. What is the kind modern word doctors use today? | Ancient | Senior | *Dementia | Sleepy
exercise: Why is calling someone «senile» as a joke unkind? | It is a polite word for a child | It means they have won a prize | *It can hurt an older person's feelings | It makes the person younger
test: Senile is which part of speech? | Adverb | *Adjective | Noun | Verb
test: «My neighbour was confused and forgot where she lived, so people said she was senile.» Which words are the clues to the meaning? | Lived and was | Said and so | Neighbour and people | *Confused and forgot where she lived
exam: Which sentence is kinder about an older person? | Senile people should stay at home | Grandad is so senile he is useless | *The doctor said Grandad has dementia, so we help him remember things | Grandad is going senile, what a joke
